"texte": [
"To the casual observer strolling through the glass-and-timber cafés of London, New York, or San Francisco, contemporary Western societies appear remarkably egalitarian. Gone are the powdered wigs of the eighteenth-century aristocracy, the rigid sumptuary laws of feudal Europe, and even the ostentatious top hats and fur coats that once announced industrial wealth from across a boulevard. Today's global elite favours organic cotton T-shirts, minimalist sneakers, and informal first-name camaraderie. Yet as an observer of hypermodern social manners, I find this egalitarian camouflage deeply revealing: far from abolishing class stratification, hypermodernity has simply replaced visible aristocratic ostentation with subtler, far more impenetrable mechanisms of cultural, cognitive, and spatial hierarchy.",
"More than a century ago, the economist Thorstein Veblen coined the phrase 'conspicuous consumption' in 'The Theory of the Leisure Class' to describe how the newly rich displayed status through lavish material luxury and ostentatious idleness. In the hypermodern era, as the sociologist Elizabeth Currid-Halkett has demonstrated, that logic has undergone a fascinating inversion: because mass production has democratised access to luxury logos and consumer gadgets, the new 'aspirational class' signals its supremacy through 'inconspicuous consumption'—investing heavily in elite early-childhood education, preventive healthcare, organic nutrition, carbon-neutral travel, and cultural capital that silently reproduce privilege across generations.",
"Pierre Bourdieu had already illuminated the deeper grammar of this social alchemy in 'Distinction', showing how dominant classes convert economic advantage into seemingly natural aesthetic taste, linguistic fluency, and intellectual ease. When social hierarchy is laundered through educational credentials and cosmopolitan sensibility, inequality ceases to look like an inherited privilege and masquerades instead as personal enlightenment.",
"It is particularly wry to observe the moral self-righteousness with which today's hypermodern meritocracy polices its boundaries: a billionaire technology founder wearing a grey crew-neck sweater lectures working-class commuters on sustainable consumption while boarding a private jet to an Alpine climate forum, blissfully convinced that his stratospheric wealth reflects pure cognitive merit rather than structural monopoly and inherited advantage.",
"The political philosopher Michael Sandel has dissected this pathology under the name 'meritocratic hubris' in 'The Tyranny of Merit': by encouraging the winners of globalised credentialism to believe they have earned their success entirely on their own, hypermodern stratification breeds an unprecedented condescension toward those without university degrees—humiliating working-class citizens while eroding the democratic solidarity required for collective self-government.",
"To critique these new hypermodern hierarchies is not, of course, to romanticise the rigid class castes of earlier centuries or to deny the genuine social mobility that public education and scientific innovation have made possible for millions.",
"Dismantling the invisible walls of hypermodern stratification requires confronting inequality at its structural roots rather than applauding superficial informality: restoring progressive taxation on extreme wealth and financial rents, reinvesting massively in universal public education and vocational dignity so that a university degree ceases to be the sole passport to social respect, and reclaiming urban public spaces from gated exclusivity—reminding ourselves that a truly democratic society is measured not by the casual dress code of its elites, but by the equal dignity it guarantees to every citizen."
],
