    "texte": [
      "Step into any contemporary metropolitan mixer, scan a professional networking platform, or browse the interface of a modern dating application, and you will witness a quiet yet profound revolution in how human beings approach one another. For millennia, human cultures drew a sacred boundary—however imperfectly observed—between the instrumental realm of commerce, where goods are priced, compared, and exchanged for utility, and the gratuitous realm of friendship, hospitality, and love, whose dignity rested precisely on their resistance to calculation. Today, that boundary is dissolving at breakneck speed: under the relentless expansion of market logic into the most intimate corners of private life, human relationships themselves are increasingly experienced, evaluated, and managed like financial assets in a speculative portfolio.",
      "The sociologist Eva Illouz has dissected this mutation with surgical precision under the name 'emotional capitalism'. As market rationality colonises romantic and social life, individuals are trained to view themselves as products competing in an endless marketplace of attractiveness, curating profiles, optimising emotional returns, and discarding partners the moment an encounter ceases to deliver frictionless gratification. What is lost in this transactional choreography is the slow, unpredictable alchemy of mutual vulnerability through which two imperfect people learn to care for one another across time.",
      "In 'What Money Can't Buy', the political philosopher Michael Sandel warned that when market norms invade non-market spheres—from paying strangers to queue in line or ghostwrite personal wedding toasts to hiring professional 'rent-a-friend' companions—they do not merely distribute services more efficiently; they actively corrupt the moral meaning of the practice itself. A gesture of affection or loyalty that can be outsourced or priced loses the very quality of uncoerced devotion that made it precious.",
      "It is particularly wry to observe the managerial vocabulary with which hypermodern professionals now narrate their personal lives: one no longer makes friends over shared curiosity or accidental kindness, one 'cultivates strategic social capital'; one does not console a grieving colleague out of simple human decency, one 'practices empathetic leadership'; and one does not fall unpredictably in love, one 'vets high-value relational matches' according to pre-screened compatibility metrics.",
      "More than a century ago, the philosopher Martin Buber articulated the existential gulf separating instrumental manipulation from genuine human encounter in 'I and Thou'. When we relate to another person in the mode of 'I-It', we treat them as an object to be classified, utilised, or consumed for our own advantage; only when we risk entering the mode of 'I-Thou'—meeting the other as an irreducible, mysterious subject beyond all calculation—does authentic human presence come into being.",
      "To critique the marketisation of intimacy is not, of course, to deny that economic security matters in human partnerships or to romanticise eras when marriage was an explicit financial treaty arranged by families without regard for personal affection.",
      "Reclaiming the authenticity of human relationships in an age of pervasive commodification therefore requires deliberate cultural and civic resistance: refusing the utilitarian jargon of 'personal branding' and 'networking' in our private lives, safeguarding public spaces and associative traditions—from community kitchens to amateur arts clubs—where people meet outside commercial transactions, and remembering that the deepest bonds of human life are never investments yielding predictable dividends, but gifts of gratuitous presence."
