    "texte": [
      "Among the hardest-won achievements of modern constitutional democracy is the principle of civic tolerance: the commitment to protect peaceful coexistence among citizens holding deeply divergent religious, philosophical, and political convictions. Forged in the aftermath of Europe's devastating wars of religion by thinkers from John Locke to Voltaire, tolerance rests upon the noble refusal to coerce conscience through state violence. Yet in an era marked by militant authoritarian movements, coordinated digital hate campaigns, and ideological groups that openly exploit democratic freedoms in order to dismantle democracy itself, pluralistic societies confront an acute constitutional dilemma: how far can a free society extend tolerance to those whose explicit project is the destruction of tolerance?",
      "In a famous footnote to 'The Open Society and Its Enemies' published in 1945, the philosopher Karl Popper formulated this dilemma as the 'paradox of tolerance'. Unlimited tolerance, Popper warned, must inevitably lead to the disappearance of tolerance itself: if a society extends unconditional protection to intolerant ideologies without defending its constitutional order, the tolerant will be destroyed, and tolerance with them. Accordingly, an open society must reserve the ultimate right to suppress movements that refuse rational debate and incite followers to answer arguments with fists, pistols, or systematic persecution.",
      "In 'A Theory of Justice', John Rawls refined Popper's insight with rigorous constitutional precision, distinguishing between intolerant beliefs—which a just society must endure so long as they pose no imminent threat to equal liberty—and intolerant conduct that actively seeks to strip fellow citizens of their constitutional rights. A democracy need not panic at every offensive utterance, yet it cannot remain passively neutral when organised factions conspire to disenfranchise minorities or subvert the rule of law.",
      "It is particularly wry to observe the theatrical victimhood with which contemporary illiberal demagogues weaponise the vocabulary of free expression: after spending years calling for the censorship of independent journalists, the banning of minority voices, and the dismantling of judicial safeguards, they loudly invoke 'sacred principles of tolerance' the instant democratic institutions enforce basic laws against incitement to violence or electoral subversion.",
      "At the same time, history warns us that the power to define 'intolerance' is itself an intoxicating weapon susceptible to grave abuse. Whenever governments or digital monopolies expand vague prohibitions against 'harmful speech' or 'extremism' without strict judicial oversight, the net inevitably widens to ensnare peaceful dissidents, satirical artists, and minority critics challenging entrenched orthodoxy.",
      "Navigating the paradox of tolerance therefore demands walking a razor-thin constitutional line: neither the suicidal naivety that grants violent authoritarianism a free hand to destroy democratic institutions from within, nor the panicked authoritarianism that sacrifices civil liberties in the name of defending them.",
      "Preserving an open society capable of resisting ideological intolerance without betraying its own soul requires anchoring every restriction in transparent, narrowly tailored legal standards: guaranteeing robust protection for controversial, heterodox, and even deeply offensive ideas in public debate while drawing a bright, enforceable boundary against incitement to violence, targeted harassment, and concerted subversion of equal citizenship—and investing above all in civic education and deliberative institutions capable of defeating fanaticism through the patient force of reason."
