"texte": [
"Mr President, Excellencies, Distinguished Ambassadors, Ladies and Gentlemen, built from the ashes of two catastrophic world wars in 1945, the modern multilateral system embodied humanity's most audacious institutional wager: that sovereign nations could replace the law of the jungle with collective deliberation, binding international treaties, and shared responsibility for peace and human dignity. Eighty years later, that noble architecture stands visibly strained to the breaking point. From deadlocked Security Council resolutions over devastating armed conflicts to stalled climate finance negotiations and vaccine nationalism during global pandemics, multilateralism appears trapped in an acute structural contradiction: entrusted with solving planetary crises that no single state can master alone, international institutions remain hostage to the narrow short-term national interests of the very member states that created them.",
