"texte": [
"Across the history of modern diplomacy, few concepts have proven simultaneously as indispensable and as dangerously ambiguous as 'collective security'. Conceived after the carnage of the twentieth century's world wars to deter aggression through the solemn pledge that an attack against one member of an alliance constitutes an attack against all, collective security arrangements have undeniably preserved peace across major regions for decades. Yet in an era of renewed great-power rivalry, maritime chokepoint disputes, and competing military blocs, the boundary separating legitimate collective self-defence from geopolitical expansionism has grown perilously blurred—raising the urgent question of when defensive alliances cease to stabilise an international order and begin inadvertently to provoke the very conflicts they were designed to prevent.",
"In 1950, the international relations scholar John Herz diagnosed this tragic perceptual trap as the 'security dilemma'. In an international system lacking a supreme global arbiter, steps taken by one state or alliance to enhance its own security—expanding military bases, deploying missile shields, or incorporating new buffer states—are almost invariably perceived by rival powers not as benign defensive precautions, but as menacing offensive encirclement, prompting counter-armaments that leave every party less secure than before.",
"The realist historian E. H. Carr had already warned in 'The Twenty Years' Crisis' that dominant powers routinely conflate their own strategic expansion with universal peace and international morality. When an alliance defines its 'vital security perimeter' thousands of miles beyond its homeland borders while condemning any comparable security concern expressed by rival nations as illegitimate revisionism, collective security quietly mutates into unilateral sphere-of-influence hegemony.",
