"texte": [
"Mr President, Excellencies, Distinguished Delegates, Ladies and Gentlemen, we gather at a moment of profound institutional reckoning for the international community. Across every domain of planetary interdependence—from climate stabilisation, biodiversity preservation, and pandemic preparedness to artificial intelligence governance, sovereign debt relief, and nuclear arms control—the demand for coordinated global governance has never been more urgent. Yet at this very hour of maximum systemic peril, the international institutions forged in the mid-twentieth century appear visibly weakened, underfunded, and bypassed—caught in a widening chasm between twenty-first-century planetary challenges and nineteenth-century habits of fragmented national sovereignty.",
"Why have global institutions suffered such a steady erosion of authority and public confidence? First and foremost, because their governance structures remain frozen in the geopolitical power distribution of 1945. When the United Nations Security Council, the International Monetary Fund, and the World Bank deny equitable decision-making weight to entire continents representing the majority of humanity, those institutions inevitably forfeit moral legitimacy across the Global South and invite the proliferation of rival parallel forums.",
"Second, as the political philosopher David Held argued in his work on 'cosmopolitan democracy', global governance suffers from an acute democratic deficit: critical decisions affecting workers, farmers, and ecosystems worldwide are negotiated behind closed doors by unaccountable technocrats and corporate lobbyists, leaving ordinary citizens feeling alienated from distant institutions that appear responsive to financial markets rather than human dignity.",
"It is particularly wry to observe the circular cynicism with which certain powerful governments systematically starve international agencies of regular assessed funding, block the appointment of appellate judges to global trade courts, and ignore international tribunal rulings—and then point triumphantly to the resulting institutional paralysis as 'proof' that multilateral governance is inherently dysfunctional.",
