"To diagnose the crisis of global governance is not, Ladies and Gentlemen, to advocate an unaccountable world super-state that would erase national democracies or cultural diversity. Healthy global governance does not replace local self-government; it empowers communities by solving the cross-border problems that no single nation can tackle alone.",
"Therefore, before this assembly, I call for a bold constitutional renewal of global governance anchored in subsidiarity, equity, and democratic accountability: democratising the voting structures of the United Nations and international financial institutions so every region holds a fair voice; establishing binding global regulatory frameworks and independent scientific oversight for artificial intelligence, climate commons, and sovereign debt restructuring; and opening international institutions to structured participation by parliaments, municipal networks, scientific academies, and civil society—so that global governance ceases to be a fragile club of rival states and becomes the resilient institutional guardian of humanity's shared future."
