    "texte": [
      "For more than two centuries, modern economic orthodoxy has rested upon an axiom treated with the reverence of a natural law: the belief that human welfare depends upon the perpetual, exponential expansion of material production and capital accumulation. Measured by the relentless ascent of Gross Domestic Product, this growth imperative undeniably lifted hundreds of millions of people out of ancestral destitution during the industrial era. Yet in the twenty-first century, the foundational premise of unlimited accumulation has collided head-on with an immovable physical reality: an economic system predicated upon infinite compound expansion cannot endure indefinitely inside a finite biosphere governed by strict thermodynamic and ecological boundaries.",
      "As early as 1971, the pioneering ecological economist Nicholas Georgescu-Roegen demonstrated in 'The Entropy Law and the Economic Process' that standard neoclassical models commit a fatal category error by treating the economy as an isolated circular flow of money between firms and households, wholly detached from material reality. Every industrial production cycle irreversibly converts low-entropy natural resources—concentrated minerals, fertile topsoil, fossil energy, and intact aquifers—into high-entropy waste, atmospheric carbon, and thermal dissipation that cannot simply be conjured back into existence by market price signals.",
      "Building upon this biophysical realism, the economist Herman Daly proposed the concept of a 'steady-state economy', distinguishing sharply between quantitative 'growth'—the physical enlargement of resource throughput—and qualitative 'development', which signifies the improvement of human wellbeing, knowledge, artistic richness, and social equity within stable ecological limits. While a living organism grows rapidly during youth before maturing into healthy dynamic equilibrium, an economy addicted to endless physical enlargement behaves less like a thriving organism than like a malignant cell.",
