"texte": [
"INTERVIEWER: Professor Miriam Vance, sociologist of work and labour markets, for several decades after 1945, post-war industrial democracies built social stability around the standard full-time employment contract—complete with predictable wages, paid leave, collective bargaining, and pension rights. Why has structural precarisation—zero-hours contracts, bogus self-employment, gig-platform piecework, and chronic job insecurity—become a defining feature of post-industrial economies today?",
"PROFESSOR VANCE: Because employers and financial markets systematically shifted economic risk off corporate balance sheets and onto the shoulders of individual workers. Under the banner of 'labour market flexibility', firms dismantled long-term employment commitments in favour of just-in-time staffing—allowing companies to summon human labour by the minute via smartphone apps while disclaiming responsibility for sickness, parental leave, training, or old-age security.",
"INTERVIEWER: The economist Guy Standing famously termed this expanding class 'the precariat'—people living in permanent occupational insecurity without a stable career narrative or social safety net. How does algorithmic management on digital platforms intensify that precarisation?",
"PROFESSOR VANCE: Algorithmic management combines the worst features of nineteenth-century piecework with twenty-first-century panoptic surveillance! A delivery courier or ride-hail driver works under an invisible automated boss that dynamically lowers pay rates, assigns arbitrary customer ratings, and can 'deactivate' a worker's livelihood instantaneously without human appeal—all while corporate lawyers insist the worker is an 'independent entrepreneur'.",
