      "Convening at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology for a public forum on artificial intelligence and economic justice, the labour economist Professor Marcus Sterling and the political philosopher Dr Elena Rostova confronted a defining challenge of twenty-first-century political economy: how can democratic societies distribute wealth equitably when frontier automation and generative artificial intelligence increasingly decouple productivity growth from human labour? 'For nearly two centuries, industrial democracies relied upon the wage contract as their primary engine of redistribution,' Professor Sterling began. 'As machines boosted worker productivity, trade unions and social legislation ensured that rising output translated into rising household incomes. Today, however, when algorithms and autonomous robotics can perform cognitive, legal, diagnostic, and logistical tasks at near-zero marginal cost, the labour share of national income risks shrinking dramatically while astronomical returns accrue to the tiny oligopoly owning the underlying models and compute infrastructure.'",
