"texte": [
"Across vast stretches of Africa, Latin America, and Southeast Asia, an enduring economic paradox continues to perplex observers of global development: nations blessed with abundant reserves of cobalt, lithium, copper, petroleum, timber, and fertile agricultural land frequently suffer from fragile public infrastructure, acute social inequality, and persistent macroeconomic vulnerability. Far from serving as an automatic springboard to broad-based prosperity, reliance upon exporting unprocessed raw commodities locks many economies of the Global South into an 'extractive model'—a structural configuration whose historical roots stretch back to colonial trade routes and whose contradictions are intensifying amid the twenty-first-century green energy transition.",
"Political economists have long analysed this pathology under the name 'the resource curse', or what the scholar Terry Lynn Karl termed 'the paradox of plenty'. When state revenues depend predominantly upon enclave oil or mining royalties rather than broad-based domestic taxation, governing elites face weakened incentives to build accountable democratic institutions or diversify local industry—while volatile international commodity prices subject national budgets to devastating boom-and-bust cycles.",
"As the dependency theorists Raúl Prebisch and Samir Amin demonstrated decades ago, the extractive economy is further penalised by structural asymmetries in global trade: raw ores and unrefined crops capture only a tiny fraction of final retail value, whereas high-margin refining, battery manufacturing, and brand distribution remain concentrated in industrialised centres—perpetuating an unequal exchange that drains ecological wealth out of the Global South while leaving behind polluted watersheds and depleted soils.",
"It is particularly wry to observe the green moralism with which wealthy industrialised nations now urge resource-rich developing countries to accelerate the extraction of lithium, cobalt, and rare earths to power electric vehicles and wind turbines in the Global North—while opposing local industrial policies, technology transfers, or export restrictions that would allow those producing nations to refine minerals and manufacture batteries domestically.",
"Moreover, modern extractive enclaves often operate as fenced-off economic islands connected by dedicated railways and ports directly to foreign markets, generating few backward linkages with local small businesses, agricultural producers, or technical universities.",
"To critique the contradictions of the extractive development model is not, of course, to suggest that resource-rich nations should leave their mineral wealth untouched while millions lack electricity, clinics, and schools. Natural endowments can become a genuine blessing when governed as sovereign national commons.",
"Breaking free from the extractive trap in the Global South therefore requires transforming raw geological wealth into broad-based industrial and human capability: enacting transparent resource-governance laws and sovereign stabilisation funds to insulate budgets from commodity volatility; imposing progressive local-processing mandates and regional industrial coalitions so minerals are refined and manufactured into finished goods on domestic soil; and investing resource revenues directly into universal education, agroecology, and clean public infrastructure—ensuring that natural wealth builds lasting sovereign prosperity rather than fleeting enclave rents."
