"texte": [
"For generations of economists, policymakers, and citizens across the modern world, 'prosperity' and 'material economic growth' have functioned as virtually interchangeable synonyms. Whenever quarterly Gross Domestic Product rises, commentators celebrate national vitality; whenever output slows or stabilises, headlines warn darkly of stagnation and social crisis. Yet as humanity confronts breached planetary boundaries, accelerating biodiversity collapse, and widespread psychological burnout within affluent consumer societies, a profound philosophical and economic question demands our attention: can genuine human prosperity be conceived, measured, and sustained without perpetual material growth?",
"As far back as 1848, in his 'Principles of Political Economy', the classical philosopher and economist John Stuart Mill challenged the assumption that endless material expansion was the ultimate goal of human civilisation. Mill looked forward to a 'stationary state' of capital and population—not as an era of gloomy deprivation, but as an emancipatory cultural threshold in which human beings, freed from the frantic struggle for competitive accumulation, could devote their energies to intellectual inquiry, artistic creation, moral cultivation, and the unhurried enjoyment of nature.",
"In 'Prosperity Without Growth', the ecological economist Tim Jackson deepened Mill's vision for the twenty-first century, arguing that authentic prosperity has far less to do with ever-expanding flows of disposable consumer goods than with our capability to flourish as human beings within ecological limits—enjoying meaningful work, secure health, strong community bonds, and a sense of shared civic purpose.",
"It is particularly wry to observe the accounting absurdities embedded in Gross Domestic Product, the supreme scorecard of growth-obsessed economies: when an oil tanker spills toxic crude along a coastline, when chronic stress drives millions to purchase antidepressants, or when planned obsolescence forces households to replace durable appliances every three years, GDP dutifully registers every cleanup contract, medical bill, and replacement purchase as a glorious increase in national 'prosperity'.",
"The Nobel laureate economist Amartya Sen has provided a rigorous philosophical foundation for transcending this material fetishism through his 'capabilities approach': commodities and income are never ends in themselves, but mere instruments whose true value depends on whether they expand people's substantive freedoms to lead lives they have reason to value—freedoms rooted in health, education, political participation, cultural expression, and unhurried time.",
"To conceive of prosperity without endless material growth is not, however, to ignore the urgent material needs of billions of people across the Global South—or impoverished citizens in wealthy nations—who still require substantial expansion of clean energy, housing, healthcare, and transport infrastructure. Post-growth prosperity applies above all to over-consuming affluent economies that must contract their ecological footprint while redistributing wealth fairly.",
"Reimagining prosperity beyond material growth therefore requires a courageous transformation of both economic institutions and cultural imagination: replacing GDP with comprehensive dashboards of human wellbeing, leisure time, and ecological regeneration; shifting investment from disposable consumer manufacturing toward low-carbon care, education, arts, and ecosystem restoration; and rediscovering the ancient philosophical truth that true wealth lies not in multiplying endless material wants, but in cultivating rich human relationships, shared beauty, and serene sufficiency."
],
